==English==

===Etymology===
The [[#Adjective|adjective]] is first attested in 1471, in {{w|Middle English}}, the [[#Verb|verb]] in 1533, the [[#Noun|noun]] in 1941; partly {{inh+|en|enm|iterat|iterat(e)|pos=adjective|nocap=1}}, partly {{bor+|en|la|iterātus|nocap=1}}, {{glossary|perfect}} {{glossary|passive}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|la|iterō||to do something for a second time, repeat}} (see {{m|en|-ate|pos=etymology 1, 2 and 3}}) , from {{af|la|iterum|-ō|t1=again|id2=denominative|nocat=1}}. Sporadical participial usage of the [[#Adjective|adjective]] up until {{w|Early Modern English}}.

===Pronunciation===
; verb
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɪtəɹeɪt/|/ˈaɪtəɹeɪt/|a=UK}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-iterate.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɪtəɹeɪt/|[ˈɪɾəɹeɪt]|a=US,CA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɪtəɹæɪt/|[ˈɪɾəɹæɪt]|a=AU}}
; noun
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɪtərət/}}

===Verb===
{{wikidata lexeme|L26}}
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|computing}} To perform or repeat an action on each item in a set.
#: {{syn|en|reiterate|Thesaurus:reiterate}}
#: {{ux|en|The max() function '''iterates''' through the data to find the highest value.}}
# {{lb|en|computing|mathematics}} To perform or repeat an action on the results of each such prior action.
#: {{ux|en|In mathematics, an '''iterated''' function is a function which is composed with itself, possibly ad infinitum, in a process called iteration.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To utter or do a second time or many times; to repeat.
#: {{ux|en|to '''iterate''' advice}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=8|passage=Nor Eve to '''iterate''' / Her former trespass feared.|year=1873}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1842|author=William Leman Rede|title=The Royal Rake: and the Adventures of Alfred Chesterton|page=25
|passage="Where's your money?" Jack exclaimed, hoarsely, in a well-feigned voice.<br>"Ah! where's the [[rowdy]]?" '''iterated''' Clayton, in a tone it was impossible to conceal.}}
# {{lb|en|design|ambitransitive}} To [[repeat]] an activity, making [[incremental]] changes each time.
#* {{RQ:Time|year=December 13 2021|author=Molly Ball; Jeffrey Kluger; Alejandro de la Garza|title=Elon Musk: Person of the Year 2021|url=https://time.com/person-of-the-year-2021-elon-musk/|text=For NASA and most private aerospace companies, a single crash is a setback that can take years to recover from. SpaceX works more like a Silicon Valley startup, where the goal is to fail quickly and '''iterate'''.|accessdate=31 December 2021|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20211213124147/https://time.com/person-of-the-year-2021-elon-musk/|archivedate=13 December 2021}}

